src/backend/access/prolly/README

Prolly storage
==============

This directory holds version control for Postgres: tables that can be
branched, committed, diffed, merged, cloned, pushed and pulled.

A database's versioned schemas (CREATE SCHEMA ... VERSIONED) are the part
under version control.  Their tables use the table access method "prolly"
(prolly_table.c): heap's pages, read and written by heap's code, kept in
block-mapped storage (storage/smgr/bmap.c), so each branch's copy of a
table shares its pages copy-on-write with the others.  That working set is
ordinary Postgres: MVCC, WAL, indexes, constraints and triggers all work
as they do on heap.

A commit folds the session branch's versioned schemas into canonical,
content-addressed prolly trees, kept in the database's chunk store, and
names the canonical root (canon_folded.c).  Diffs and merges compare
those trees; remotes exchange their chunks.

Each versioned object has catalog rows on every branch that has used it,
each carrying its branch (catalog/branch.c).  Every branch-aware catalog
has an index on its branch column alone (pg_shdepend one on its database
and branch), and what reads a branch's objects reads them by it
(catalog_branch_beginscan()): a branch's first use copies the rows on its
source, a branch's delete removes the rows on it, and a commit's
canonical form, and the checks of a working set against a commit, read
the rows on the session's branch.  That work is in the objects on the
branch, however many rows other branches and shared objects have.  One
catalog is still read whole for the canonical form: pg_cast
(describe_casts()), since a cast between a type an extension has in a
versioned schema and a type outside it, made outside the extension, is
shared yet described; pg_cast grows only with user casts.


Interfaces and implementations
------------------------------

The current engine uses the following interfaces and registered components.
These describe the existing code; there is no plan to build alternative engines.
Changes to this design are tracked in GitHub issues:

	Interface         Header                  Implementations
	chunker           prolly_chunker.h        bytes, stub
	content_hash      prolly_hash.h           blake3, sha256, stub
	key_encoder       prolly_keyenc.h         ordered, stub
	value_encoder     prolly_valenc.h         canonical, stub
	reclamation       prolly_reclaim.h        copying, stub
	refs              prolly_refs.h           copies, stub
	transfer          prolly_transfer.h       chunks, stub
	diff_merge        prolly_merge.h          folded, stub
	tree              prolly_tree.h           cow, stub
	table_keying      prolly_keying.h         pk_or_prefix, stub
	canonicalizer     prolly_canon.h          folded, stub
	chunk_store       prolly_chunkstore.h     journal
	chunk_format      prolly_chunkformat.h    basic
	row_applier       prolly_applier.h        replication, stub
	table_file        prolly_tablefile.h      basic, stub
	change_finder     prolly_changes.h        pages, log, stub
	change_log        prolly_changelog.h      relation, stub
	remote            prolly_remote.h         file
	value_memo        prolly_memo.h           shared, stub
	writers           prolly_writers.h        modes, stub
	move_planner      prolly_moveplan.h       costs, stub
	matcache          prolly_matcache.h       branches, stub

An interface header declares a routine struct: a ProllyImplInfo (interface,
name, impl_id, format_version) followed only by callback pointers, with the
contract written as comments.  An implementation is one or more source files
here, named <interface>_<implementation>.c, that define a const routine
struct.  Nothing else includes an implementation's private headers or calls
its functions.

Callers go through PROLLY_CALL(routine, callback, args...) (prolly_impl.h).
A NULL callback means "not implemented", and PROLLY_CALL raises
ERRCODE_FEATURE_NOT_SUPPORTED naming the interface, implementation and
callback.  So a stub is a routine with every callback NULL, and a real
implementation can be filled in one callback at a time.

Built-in implementations are listed in src/include/access/prolly_impllist.h,
the one place that names them, and found by interface and name through the
registry (prolly_registry.c).  Libraries in shared_preload_libraries can
register more with prolly_register_impl().  Interfaces keep their numbers,
so the enum has gaps where interfaces were removed;
prolly_interface_valid() says which numbers are interfaces.

Which implementation is used comes from configuration (prolly_config.c),
never from code: a prolly table's history is folded with its access
method's profile (keying, encoders, hash, chunker, trees), and a
database's version control is its profile (refs, transfer, diff and
merge, canonicalizer).


Canonical values
----------------

Rows and keys are folded in canonical form: the same bytes on every
platform, in every cluster and session, whatever the table's DDL history,
with no local OIDs.  prolly_datum.c holds the canonical encoding of single
values, shared by the key and value encoders as a fixed part of the format:
send formats produced with the client encoding fixed to UTF-8
(SetClientEncodingOverride in mbutils.c), and OID-free encodings of arrays
and composites.  valenc_canonical.c stores live columns only, without
trailing NULLs, and decodes rows encoded under an earlier layout.

A reader that needs only some columns asks for those (the value encoder's
decode_columns(), the keying's decode_row_columns()): the others are
skipped by their length prefixes, so no type decoder runs for them and no
long value of theirs is read from its tree.  fields() gives each column's
encoded bytes, or its value tree's reference, without decoding anything,
for callers that compare columns by their bytes (two rows hold a column
alike exactly when its bytes are the same) and decode only some
(decode_field()).  pg_stat_prolly counts the columns decoded
(columns_decoded) and the long values read whole (values_read,
value_bytes_read).

Folded format 17 (key/value encoder version 6) normalizes binary I/O for
extension base types and for ranges whose bounds reach one (including
through a composite or array subtype): send(receive(send(value))).
Arrays, composites and domains normalize their children recursively.  The
fixed built-in codecs keep their existing bytes and avoid this extra work.
This makes a value's stored bytes match its reconstructed representation:
for example, PostGIS can read either signed-NaN POINT spelling as POINT EMPTY.
As with binary dump/restore, reconstruction preserves the receiver's value;
it does not promise to preserve a physical spelling that receive discards.
Working-side cell merges fold and decode values before comparison, so the
same normalization applies there.

A normalized primary key must compare equal to its original value under
the actual key operator class.  Encoding rejects values that cross that
boundary, including during writes and adding a key to existing rows.  This
keeps the tree's key compatible with native index lookups into the heap.
Reconstruction also re-inserts rows through the table's other unique and
exclusion indexes, so a value such an index constrains is held to the same
rule: equal under a unique btree key column's operator class, and otherwise
(an exclusion operator, an index expression or predicate) unchanged by the
round trip.  Writes and building such an index over existing rows refuse
values that would not reconstruct.  Other non-key values may normalize
without that restriction.  Extension I/O must
still be stable after reconstruction; testing one value is not a certificate
that an arbitrary extension's send/receive functions satisfy that contract.

An extension's receive may be stricter than its input: PostGIS's text input
takes a one-point LINESTRING or an unclosed ring that its receive refuses.
Such a value, which a table may already hold, is kept as its text form
(tag 1) instead.  Only a data exception (class 22), or an internal error
raised by the type's own code rather than the server's (by dladdr(): an
error from a static or inlined server function is taken as the type's,
and on Windows every internal error is), counts as a refusal; anything else (a cancel, a deadlock, a lock not available, a
catalog lookup failing) is raised, so a value has one form fold after fold
and whatever it is folded with.  The text must read back as a value the type
sends as it sends this one, and the value is stored as its text reads
back: a geometry with a cached bounding box reads back without the cache,
which its send form doesn't hold.  As
receive is the extension's code, a refusal is caught in a subtransaction:
a fold encodes each table's rows as one batch (prolly_datum_batch()), with
receive unprotected inside one subtransaction, and a refusal rolls the
batch back and runs it again with a subtransaction per value.  Encoding
outside a batch takes one per value.  Not caught: in a parallel worker (a
parallel index build), where no subtransaction can be begun; while the
caller holds off interrupts (a buffer's content lock, a critical
section), so encoders read a page's copy, not the page; and for a range
over a row type holding such a type, whose values are untagged;
there a refusal is raised as before.  Text forms are written and read
with extra_float_digits, DateStyle, IntervalStyle, bytea_output (hex),
TimeZone (UTC), timezone_abbreviations (Default) and lc_monetary, lc_numeric
and lc_time (C) fixed, so a value's canonical form doesn't depend on the
committer's session (since folded format 20).  Fixing them costs a GUC nest
level and a setting changed for each that differs, so loops over many
values fix them once (prolly_datum_fixed_text()): a fold's batch, a change
finder's walk of a table (a status, a diff), the rows a reset or merge
writes (as a restore runs under the settings its dump fixes), and a row's
values checked as it is written.


Extension ordering and history compatibility
-------------------------------------------

Folded formats since 16 record the extensions ordering each table's primary key,
including comparators reached through arrays, composites, ranges, multiranges
and domains.  A dependency's optional flags have one order: "versioned", then
"ordering".  Clone and move targets require the exact recorded ordering
versions; ordinary dependencies continue to require a compatible minimum.
A move checks the target, not the old head that it replaces.

Historical reads and merges check ordering per table.  After an extension
upgrade, a table whose definition and row types still match can be read by
re-sorting its encoded keys under the installed comparator.  This spills at
maintenance_work_mem and preserves row/value bytes and long-value references;
no commit is rewritten.  A concurrent definition or row-type change is
refused for that table.  Unrelated history remains readable.  Upgrades must
still preserve the ability to decode the stored values; there is no general
extension binary-compatibility certificate.  Re-sort results are not cached.

An upgrade invalidates baseline ordering and the row-only autocommit shortcut,
including a legacy head missing ordering flags.  Rebuild native indexes when
the extension's comparator changes, then commit to record its new ordering.
Hard reset to a target recording the old version remains refused (including
HEAD); a forward reset to a compatible new target is allowed.

This is a deliberate history format change.  Earlier servers cannot read
format-17 commits, so these commits must not be
sent to an older server or used after downgrading.  Current code still parses
legacy dependency entries, but it does not retroactively certify legacy key
ordering.  Older root formats retain the existing recommit requirement;
committing a branch writes its current working state in the new format.


Extension configuration rows
----------------------------

An extension's configuration tables (pg_extension.extconfig, such as
PostGIS's spatial_ref_sys) are shared by every branch and aren't
versioned, but the commits' data may refer to their rows (a column of a
custom SRID).  So since folded format 19 an extension's dependency
records, after its flags, what pg_dump dumps with it (prolly_extconfig.c):
each row its extcondition selects (a user's, not its script's), column by
column by name, each value in its canonical form, so a row is recorded the
same whatever the committer's settings; and the state of each
configuration sequence, and of each sequence a configuration table owns
(an identity's, a serial's), once it has moved from its start.  They are
read as the tables' owners with row security off, a search path of
pg_catalog alone and fixed text settings, as pg_dump reads them, so
nothing in the committer's schemas runs as the owner.  An extension
without such rows is recorded as before.  A clone writes the rows its
configuration tables lack into the extension it makes (by column name,
overriding identities, generated columns left to be generated, through
one prepared INSERT per table) and sets its sequences, as a restore of a
dump does.  A move (checkout, reset, merge, cherry-pick, revert) to
commits recording a row the database lacks is refused, naming the table;
rows the database has besides are its own.  A table with a primary key
(or a replica identity index) has its rows matched by key, so a row whose
other columns were edited since is still there; heads recording one key
with different values give a clone the checked-out branch's row, with a
warning naming the table and the key.  A clone's writes check its
extension's domains (on a column, in an array or a row) and lengths, as a
restore's input would.  A keyless table's
rows are matched whole, each as many times as recorded.  An element
naming anything but one of the extension's configuration tables (or a
sequence one owns) is refused, nothing an element holds is put in a query
as text, and decoded text is checked to be valid in the database's
encoding, as text's receive checks it.  The dependency tree is taken from
the backend's cache only while a digest of the configuration tables
matches: each visible row's place and inserting transaction, read without
encoding any row.


Identity across renames
-----------------------

A table or column keeps its identity when it is renamed: the name it was
made with, and which of the objects made with that name it is (0 unless
another holding that identity was there, as a renamed column is when a
new one takes its old name).  A root holds no identities, so the same
tables, columns and rows give the same root however they were named
before.  A commit records its root's identities beside it (its "identity"
item): only those that differ from the object's own name with 0, so a
commit whose objects were never renamed records nothing.  Commit ids already
depend on history, through their parents and metadata; two commits with
the same root can still record different identities.

A branch's working set finds its identities through its own relations
and attribute numbers, which a rename keeps and a drop and an add don't.
The refs keep an anchor for each branch ('I'), written by each commit and
after each move.  The anchor is valid only for the head it was made at.
Each live table and column is found in the anchor; one the anchor hasn't
is new since and gets its own name's identity (the first of that name's
that no live object holds).  Without an anchor of the head (the branch
moved without one made), each is found by its name in the head's identity
record.  So a column dropped and another of its name added gets the
dropped column's identity if that was its own name's, as Dolt's tags do,
but never the identity of a column renamed into the name.  Identities are
read from the catalogs, without opening (or locking) any relation, and a
read of one table identifies that table alone.

Reads match versions by identity.  dolt_at, dolt_history_table,
dolt_diff_table, row diffs, and a merge's conflict listing read each
version's columns under the names the working set gives the same columns.
Diffs read its tables that way too, and a summary (the canonicalizer's
changes()) pairs a table renamed as one renamed table, its rows and
definition perhaps changed too.  dolt_at and dolt_history_table read a
version's table by its name, as Dolt's AS OF and history do.  Tables
alike but unrelated are never paired.  The canonicalizer's renames()
works out the renames and rename_description() applies them in memory,
so nothing is written.  A merge names base, ours and theirs as one, using
merge_renames().  An
object renamed on one side takes that side's name.  Renamed differently
on both sides, or two kept objects given one name, is a schema conflict.
The branch's own tables and columns are renamed to the merged names first
(rename_objects(), ALTER TABLE ... RENAME), so their rows stay where they
are and only the other side's changes are written.  The anchor is then the
merge's.

A history read of a table's rows (dolt_at, dolt_history_table, dolt_diff,
dolt_diff_table, by row type or by name) takes SELECT on the table here
of the version's name, and that grant covers only the versions that are
that table, as relation-made history: every commit this database makes of
a branch records the relations its working set's tables were ('L', refs
format 14: each table's OID and qualified name), and a version is the
table's when its commit was made from the table's relation, reached back
from the working set through the head and each commit's parents, a step
taken only where both commits were made from it (its name at each, as
the record has it, so a rename is followed), or forward from one of them
along another branch's commits made from it.  A version of another table
of its name, one dropped before this one was made, is read only by the
database's owner or a member of pg_read_all_data, as is a commit without
a record: one made in another database (fetched, pulled, cloned or pushed
here).  Commits name tables by name and identity alone, and a re-made
table keeps its name's identity (#891), so the names can't tell a head
moved to a commit holding a dropped table and then committed, a merge of
a branch still holding it, a squash of the drop into the re-make, a
table dropped and made again within one commit, or a branch made at the
old table's commit from the new one; the records can.  A commit that
isn't its working set folded (a merge's, a pick's or a revert's, over
uncommitted changes) is recorded made only from the relations its first
parent's record and the working set both have, as it holds a table
dropped and made again in the working set as the parent had it.  What
the records can't: a branch's first use may
make its copies from another branch's relation (the table here's, moved
to the old rows), and its commits are then that relation's; a move that
brings the old table's rows (a merge, a hard reset, a revert of the drop,
a cherry-pick) writes them into the new table, whose rows they then are
(#936); a table a merge makes here is a relation of its own, so its
branch's history isn't its grantee's here (it fails closed); and a role
given a dropped relation's OID again would read that relation's versions.
A refused read reads each commit's record about once: a walk that meets
nothing marks the commits it saw apart.  Moving a head takes a write role
(see Writers), not SELECT alone.  A plan's row estimate of a version, and
dolt_diff_stat's counts, are read on the same rule.

Folded format 18 records identities in commits.  Formats are not kept
compatible while the prototype iterates: this build reads, moves to and
merges format 18 alone, and a root of format 17 or older has its tables'
rows unreadable and is refused for moves and merges ("a commit made in an
older format of prolly's history can't be ..."); commit the branch again to
write it in format 18.  A server of an earlier format refuses format 18
likewise, rather than matching renamed objects by name.


Internal DDL and event triggers
------------------------------

Checkout, reset, merge, cherry-pick, clone and other internal history work
reproduce database state without firing user event triggers.  Both SPI DDL
and direct catalog deletions use branch_writes_hush()'s existing nested,
subtransaction-aware scope.  Event-trigger dispatch and command/drop
collection ignore that scope, including when a move runs inside an outer
user DDL event.  The outer event retains its own commands and dropped
objects.  No session GUC is changed; normal user DDL still fires triggers
once the internal scope ends, including after errors.  User code that the
work runs within the scope (a constraint's check while verifying or
validating it, a materialized view's query, a trigger) is the user's: a
user-defined function called hushed is seen by the fmgr hook, and DDL it
runs fires event triggers and is collected as the user's own is.  An
extension's script, run to make the extension again, stays the work's.


Trees and chunks
----------------

A tree is an immutable sorted map identified by its root node
(prolly_tree.h).  tree_cow.c builds trees bottom up, cutting each level into
nodes with the chunker; the root is the first level with one node, so the
shape and every hash depend on the items alone.  apply() takes a sorted
batch of edits and re-chunks a window around each one, a level at a time,
until the new boundaries meet an old one; everything after that point is
shared with the old tree.  Nodes are chunks (chunkformat_basic.c) in the
database's chunk store (chunkstore_journal.c): an append-only journal,
later conjoined into table files (tablefile_basic.c), with the store's root
moved by commit records and its appends logged by the ProllyChunks WAL
resource manager (prolly_xlog.h).

Large values stay TOAST's in the working set.  A commit stores a column
whose canonical bytes are longer than 2048 as a reference to a value tree
of its own (valenc_canonical.c, through the tree's value_write()), so
rows holding the same value share it, and a row that is still long goes
out of line whole.  Value trees are cut by a rolling hash and addressed
by byte counts, so an insertion rewrites the chunks around it and a
slice reads only its own.  An edit can be spliced into a value tree
(the tree's value_splice()), giving the tree a write of the edited bytes
would: the leaves around the edit are cut again, and each level above
made again from the node on the edit's path until its nodes end where
the old ones did, so it reads and writes the nodes along that path and
the bytes around the edit, as a slice reads, whatever the value's size.
A leaf records the references its value holds, so walks reach those
trees without knowing the rows' layout.  The
value memo (memo_shared.c) maps a TOAST pointer a fold met to the
reference it made, so the next fold that meets the pointer (an UPDATE of
another column kept it) doesn't read the value again.


Writers
-------

Every write to a branch is noted before it is made (branch_note_write(),
catalog/branch.h): rows of prolly tables, versioned sequences, and
versioned objects' catalog rows, unless made on the session's behalf by
a version-control call.  The writers ("modes", writers_modes.c) decide
whether the write may go ahead.  In the shared mode every session writes
every branch, as in Dolt.  In the owned mode a dirty branch is one
writer's (dolt.writer, by default the session): its pg_branch row records
the owner, claimed by the first write and forgotten once a commit or
reset leaves the branch clean, and anyone else's write is refused.  A
protected branch (main in the branch-only mode, or any branch protected
by name) has no writers: only integrations change it, a merge,
cherry-pick, revert, reset or commit that commits as it changes it, as
dolt_integrate merges a writer's branch in.  In the branch-auto mode a
statement that writes, at the top level, on a protected branch first
moves the session to its writer branch (writer/<writer>/<base>), forked
from the base as the transaction's snapshot saw it and swept once it is
clean and merged.  The mode, protections and writer branches are
database settings kept in the refs.

Moving a branch's head is a write to the branch, whatever its tables'
grants: a commit (dolt_commit, or dolt.transaction_commit's at a
transaction's end), a reset of any kind (a hard reset to the head too, as
it drops the branch's changes), a merge or pull into it, a cherry-pick,
revert or rebase onto it, an integration (of the branch, and into its
target), and the branch's deletion, rename or forced move
(dolt_branch('-f', ...)).  Each takes prolly_check_head_move(): the role
has the privileges of the branch's owner, or writes the refs at large
(prolly_writes_refs(): it owns the database, has the database's CREATE
privilege, which makes branches, or has the privileges of
pg_prolly_write).  A branch's owner is the role that made it, recorded in
the refs ('A', refs format 13) as it is made, its first head set by a
fetch, clone or push included, kept by a rename and by a forced move that
makes a branch without commits again; main, a branch whose maker isn't
recorded, and one whose maker was dropped are the database owner's.
dolt_branches shows it (creator).  The record is a role's OID, not a
dependency: DROP ROLE doesn't refuse a role that owns branches (its
branches fall to the database's owner), REASSIGN OWNED and DROP OWNED
don't see them, and a role given the dropped one's OID again would own
them.  A rename's new name takes what making a branch of it takes, or
pg_prolly_write's privileges, and a rebase's own branch isn't renamed: a
rebase's branch and a writer branch are made on the session's behalf,
without the privilege to make branches, and are its role's.  In the
writer modes a writer with only its tables' grants writes and commits on
its writer branch, and integrates its own writer branch into its base if
every change the branch brings is to rows of tables it may write as they
changed (INSERT for rows added, DELETE for rows removed, UPDATE for rows
changed, as the base's catalogs grant them; the move that applies them
takes all four of a table's write privileges, as every move does).  A
change to a definition, or to rows it may not write so, and an
integration of any other branch, take the head move's privileges.

Other shared state in the refs is guarded the same way.  Making a tag, and
adding or removing a remote, takes prolly_writes_refs(); deleting a tag
takes that or having made it ('G', its maker, recorded by dolt_tag()
alone: a tag fetched or pushed is no one's).  A fetch sets the remote's
tags here only for a role that writes the refs; another's fetch leaves
them, with a notice.  A stash records the role
that pushed it; dropping or popping it takes that role's privileges or
prolly_writes_refs(), and a clear by a role without the latter clears only
its own.  dolt_serve_move_head(), a push from another database, takes
pg_prolly_push's privileges instead; deleting a branch through it is a
deletion, and takes these.

Automatic forks happen before parse analysis (including modifying CTEs)
and before cached statements reacquire relation locks. Creators therefore
wait for refs without holding write locks on the protected source that
another creator needs to copy. Analysis and cached-plan revalidation use
the writer branch's catalogs. Cleanup rechecks its candidates under refs,
so concurrent checkouts cannot delete the same completed writer twice.


Commits
-------

Table keying (keying_pkprefix.c) keys a folded table by its primary key,
or, without one, by its rows' leading bytes and content hash with a count
of identical rows.  A commit folds each table's rows into its data tree:
in full the first time, then from its baseline using the per-table change
log (the default finder "log", changes_log.c). The older "pages" finder
(changes_pages.c) remains registered. Incremental folds can fall back to a
full fold when the log or baseline cannot be used, or when more rows
changed than reading the table costs.  A table whose rows' bytes changed
shape without being written (a column added with a default, one dropped,
the key moved) is folded from its baseline's rows mapped to the shape now
only where that costs less than reading it: both read every row, and
decoding the baseline's costs more per row than scanning the heap's until
the heap holds about prolly.map_reshaped_dead_ratio (1.5, a rough
break-even) dead versions a live row, as its statistics count them (which
lag, and count none after a VACUUM or on a standby: a wrong choice costs
only time, the tree made being the same).  dolt_pending_full_folds() asks
the same.  Changes past maintenance_work_mem
are not a reason: they are sorted, spilling to disk, and applied a part at
a time in key order, so the fold's work stays that of the rows changed
(pg_stat_prolly's fold_changes_spilled).  A transaction commit's own
changes past it commit the working set instead.
The refs ("copies", refs_copies.c) keep branch heads, baselines, tags, remotes and
merge state in the chunk store's root.  Diff and merge ("folded",
diffmerge_folded.c) compare canonical trees table by table, reading only
subtrees that differ; moving a branch to another commit writes the
differences back through the row applier (applier_replication.c), as
logical replication's apply worker writes rows.  A commit of a canonical
state a caller already holds is written by the refs' commit_root(), from
the root, dependencies, parents and metadata given: it folds nothing,
reads no catalog for the commit and moves no branch.  The caller names the
commit in the same transaction: a collection after it ends drops a commit
no ref names.  commit_with() folds
the working set (and the dependencies, from the catalogs) and writes its
commit through it.

A table both sides of a merge changed in ways that don't merge (its
definitions changed differently, or one dropped it and the other changed
it, its definition or only its rows) is a schema conflict.  dolt_merge()
keeps it as the branch has it, definition and rows, records it in the
merge in progress (the refs' merge names entry, OID 0 and its names on
each side: refs format 12) and merges the other tables, as Dolt does;
dolt_schema_conflicts shows its statements in the base, the head and
theirs, each under its own name.  A table a side renamed (matched by
identity) is kept under ours' names, its own and its columns': the
merge's names are made again so (merge_renames()'s keep_ours), found by
merging the definitions once before the merge does.
dolt_conflicts_resolve() refuses it, and the merge can't be committed
until it is aborted.  A cherry-pick or revert, schema conflicts of other
objects, and a table the other side's objects use in its version (a view
over a column it added) fail as before, the last saying so.

A keyed row both sides of a merge changed is merged column by column, on
its encoded fields (the keying's row_fields()): all three versions are
rows of the table as defined now (a version of another shape is converted
first), so a column's bytes name its value.  A column whose bytes one side
left as base's takes the other's, one both changed alike keeps it, and the
merged row is assembled from the fields taken (encode_row_fields()), long
values by their references, no column decoded.  A large value neither side
changed is not fetched to find that out, nor when the merged row is
applied.  A row with a column whose base value is unsure (below) is
decoded and compared by value instead, except that where a side's long
value has base's reference neither is read (decode_row_kept()) and the
merged row keeps the reference (encode_row_kept()).  An assert-enabled
build merges each row both ways and checks they agree.

When a type change admits both text and cast readings of the base, merge
validates both interpretations before applying rows.  Each outcome walk
spools its ordered keys, conflict flags and canonical values to a temporary
BufFile.  Comparison holds one outcome from each stream and the unchanged
row, rather than two complete lists.  Outcome memory is two file buffers
plus reusable buffers sized to the largest records (and BufFile segment
metadata); temporary I/O grows with the outcomes.  Files close on success
and their resource owner removes them on error, savepoint rollback or
cancellation.  Ordinary merges do not create these files, but every such
validation creates both, however small the table, so it uses temporary
space: it counts against temp_file_limit and is reported by log_temp_files.
Reading a version as a table is defined now (as_defined_now()) keeps the
rows in their stored order when the key columns are read as they are
stored.  Otherwise (a key retyped, an enum relabelled, a keyless table) the
converted rows go to a tuplesort of their own ordered by the keying, ties
in the order read, which spills past work_mem; they are read back in order
and streamed into the tree builder, copies of a row counted and a key found
twice refused or, shown, warned of, as before.  The base's rows with an
unsure value are sorted the same way and written to a temporary file in key
order, which each merge walk reads from its start alongside theirs' changes.
While a version's rows are read, the converted rows' sort and the unsure
rows' sort can both be open, each with its own work_mem: the conversion
uses up to twice work_mem, plus each sort's merge buffers.

A merge that records constraint violations writes each table's rows as an
attempt.  Where a refused row's put-back is refused too, the table's writes
are rolled back and made again; the violations an attempt records are
spooled to a temporary file of its own, dropped when the attempt is rolled
back.  When it holds, those left are read back in order into the merge's
kept violations, passing over the handles of those forgotten (a row tried
again and written after all), which are sorted.  The rows ranked up across
attempts, and those held to be tried again while a row is pinned, are
spooled to temporary files in key order.  These files are created under the
resource owner the writes began with so that an attempt's subtransaction
can roll back without closing them; their memory is a few file buffers and
the largest row.  A keyless table's removed values, which the domain checks
look up, are held in memory up to work_mem, then sorted with a tuplesort
and written once each, in order, to an image file and a fixed-size entry
file that lookups search by halving.  All of these files close when the
table is written and go with their resource owner on error or cancellation.

A row a merge didn't write for breaking a unique index is recorded as
Dolt records it: once for each unique index it breaks, and so is each
row of the table it collides with there (the table keeping those).
Those kept are spooled to a tuplesort of their own; once the merge's
rows are written, they are matched a batch at a time, by a query per
unique index of plain columns (its predicate, if any, held by both rows;
each column compared by the index's own equality and collation, so the
index serves the lookup), against the rows
they collide with, as the merge's other checks run (seeing its writes,
as the table's owner, row security aside: an owner who can't use the
table's schema fails the merge, as its foreign key checks do), and the
violations found are kept as any other.  A refused row is looked up as
it was, read back from its row type's text kept with it (floats in
full), not from its JSON, which loses a jsonb null, an array's bounds
and a float's digits.  With NULLs not distinct, each pattern of the
refused row's NULLs (up to four key columns) is a lookup of its own, so
the index serves those too.  A refused row colliding with none now, or refused
for an index of expressions, keeps its refusal's own record.

The violations a merge keeps go to a tuplesort of their own (work_mem,
spilling past it).  To publish them, the branch's violations are visited
into the same sort, which orders them by what they are and then the
branch's first: the first of each is kept only if it is new, and the new
ones are sorted again into the order found and added to the branch's a
batch at a time (1000, or work_mem's worth of bytes up to 1MB).  A merge or
pick counts the branch's violations, to report how many it added, by
visiting them, not loading them.  The refs keep a branch's violations one entry each, keyed
by the branch and a place, after an entry holding the next place, so adding
some reads and writes none of the others (the refs' format version 2).
Format version 1 kept them all in one value under the branch's key; this
build doesn't read such a value but refuses it, with a hint to reset the
branch, which removes it.  The violations' inspection functions and the
checks before a commit still read a branch's violations whole.

The constraint checks (a merge's foreign keys, the check and not-null rows
a merge's added constraints take away, dolt_verify_constraints()) run each
query through SPI_execute_extended() with a DestReceiver that hands each
row found to its consumer as the executor returns it: the merge's
violation sort, or a list for verification, which sets the branch's
violations whole.  No query's result is held as an SPI tuple table.  The
keys of the rows changed since the head, which verification matches a
batch at a time, go to two stores shared by every table (keys now, and as
they were), each held in memory up to half of work_mem and then moved to
a temporary file; a table's keys of a side are a segment of its store, as
the diff gives a table's rows together.  What reading a table's rows takes
is released before the next table's, and each batch's JSON literal and
query are made only as that batch runs.  A merge still checks every foreign key of the tables it
wrote whole, by query; the executor's own sorts and hashes spill under
work_mem as for any query.

A commit, a checkpoint and a fold of a transaction's own rows change
only their branch's refs (its head, baseline, the names of its head's
tables, its merge state and violations, the commits its pages are kept
as of).  Every change of a branch's refs
is made holding that branch's lock to transaction end, and they take
only that, so commits on different branches run together while those on
one branch take turns.  Anything else that changes the refs (a branch
made, deleted or renamed, a reset, a merge, a push) holds the session
branch's lock (and any branch's it changes), then moves the store's root
with its transaction, as all of them did before: other such movers wait
for it.  A branch's holder makes its changes in a refs tree of its own,
rebuilt on the store's root when another's commit moved it, and moves the
root there as it commits, waiting then for a mover holding it; one that
comes to change other refs moves the root from then on.  So an operation
reading another branch's head (a branch made from it, a merge of it)
reads the head as last committed, not a commit still open there.

A move (or a first use's move of its copies) gives each table whose rows
differ from the target's the storage of another state that holds the
target's table, where sharing costs less than writing the rows (branch
operations' share(), the canonicalizer's splice_baseline()); a branch's
first use copies the branch nearest its head.  A source is a branch whose
working set is known (folded, nobody writing it since), or a kept
materialization (matcache_branches.c): the hidden branch of a detached
checkout, kept once its sessions leave, which nothing writes again.  Its
root must hold the target's table, the same data tree and description,
and its relations must be the same ones (by id) stored alike: the same
access method, columns as stored (dropped ones, and the values a short
tuple's missing columns read as, included), TOAST table and index
definitions.  Sharing a tree-mapped relation copies its root page, so its
cost doesn't grow with the table; the source is held against deletion to
the transaction's end.  A kept source is used only by a snapshot that sees
it made, so every tuple on its pages is one the snapshot sees as committed
or aborted, and only while its commit's ordering dependencies match the
installed extensions, under which its indexes were built.  pg_stat_prolly
counts the tables a move gave another state's storage (move_tables_shared)
and the tables and materialized views whose rows it wrote
(move_tables_written): a checkout's work,
table by table.

With no branch at its head, the move planner (moveplan_costs.c)
costs moving each branch's copies there, from the root its working set is
known or thought to be: each table's rows that differ, estimated from its
trees by diff_estimate() in a bounded number of nodes, and the tables
redefined, made or dropped (materialized views' rows as tables'), each
planned on its own branch.  The session's branch and at most 16 others are
weighed, each stopped once it costs more than the best yet; the cheapest is
copied, the session's on a tie.  Another branch is copied only if none of
its writers is in the middle of a change (make()'s nowait): waiting for one,
with the refs held, would hold up the session's branch's commits for as long
as that writer's transaction lasts.  Else the session's branch is copied,
waiting for its writers, as ever (pg_stat_prolly's first_use_sources_busy).
The copies are new in the transaction, so no other sees them: a table of the chosen plan whose rows differ by more
than reloading it costs (past about an eighth of them) begins empty instead
(branch operations' make(), its emptied tables: new storage, as TRUNCATE
gives it, no page shared, its change log told), and the move loads the
head's rows into it, its indexes built once after them (the row applier's
load).  Not a table with a deferrable unique, primary key or exclusion
constraint, whose rows the applier rechecks a row at a time.  A table the
head hasn't begins empty too, so the move drops it without its pages
having been shared (and, for pages no other map held, logged) first.  A
table whose definition differs keeps its pages: the move may keep its rows.

A branch's pages are kept as of the last commits it was at
(prolly.kept_commits, 2 by default): when a commit's fold, or a move
settled, leaves the working set known to be a commit's root, each
block-mapped relation stored with its tables (heaps, indexes, TOAST tables,
change logs) is kept by a snapshot of its storage (bmap_snapshot_take(),
through branch operations' keep()), O(1) in its size, a relation unchanged
since a commit kept already reusing that one's.  Only while nothing else
can be on the pages: the relations are locked against writers without
waiting, and the snapshot the commit or move went by saw every writer of
the branch that ended and nobody else is writing it; otherwise nothing is
kept.  Not at a transaction's own commit (dolt.transaction_commit), whose
head moves every transaction.  The refs record each kept commit with the
branch's baseline then and each table's relations and snapshots ('K').
A snapshot's slot names the transaction that took it, so one whose
transaction doesn't commit (a crash between the keep and the commit
included) is kept by nobody: the relation's next snapshot, or a page
sweep, lets go of it.  VACUUM leaves the pages a relation shares only
with its own snapshots as it leaves any shared page with nothing to
reclaim (an aggressive VACUUM still freezes them, copying them).  With
prolly.vacuum_copy_kept_pages on, it freezes and sets them all-visible,
copying them (the kept version goes once its commit stops being kept),
but still leaves as they are the pages another branch shares, which the
block map tells apart by when each page's entry was born (its header's
shared_below).  A share
raises shared_below; once the other branch's map is let go of, a page
sweep (prolly_page_sweep()), having counted every holder, lowers it to
the newest entry another map still holds, so VACUUM treats those pages
as unshared again.  It holds each such relation's branch meanwhile, as a
session on it does, so a branch's deletion or rename tried then fails as
if a session were on it (its detail says either may be).  A
move back to one of them (dolt_reset('--hard') to the head or the commit
before, a merge's abort, a stash) first gives each table that changed
since its pages back (restore()): new storage made of each relation's
snapshot, O(1), its pg_class row repointed with its oldest transaction ids
then, as TRUNCATE gives a relation new storage, and the old storage dropped
at commit; the baseline takes the table's entry from the kept baseline,
whose change log is restored with it, so the fold that follows reads none
of its rows.  Whatever still differs (definitions, sequences, a table not
restored) the move writes as before.  A table isn't restored if its
storage was replaced since (a rewrite, a truncation), an index was made or
dropped, it was renamed, its rows' transaction ids are older than the
database still knows, its rows in the commit can't be read here, the user
couldn't write its rows, or one of its relations is in use (its locks are
tried, not waited for): its rows are written.  Nor is one whose baseline
holds the commit's rows with fewer than prolly.restore_min_changes (2) of
them changed since, as its change finder tells from no more than that many
of its records (the log's entries, none read as a fold reads them):
restoring a table costs about as much as writing a few rows back and
syncing their pages as the commit's are kept again, so a reset of a row
writes it.  Like TRUNCATE, a restore isn't MVCC-safe: a
snapshot taken before the move commits reads the restored pages after
it.  So a small reset, its rows written, is MVCC-safe, and a large one,
restored, isn't, for a snapshot older than the move.

A branch's first use at a commit another branch keeps the pages of is
made of those pages (branch operations' make_kept()): each such table's
relations share the snapshots, O(1), and the source's writers are not
locked out (the source's relations are held with ShareUpdateExclusiveLock,
tried and never queued for, which only definition changes, VACUUM and
letting go of kept pages wait for); only relations not kept (a table made
or whose storage was replaced since, a sequence) are copied as before,
under locks against writers.  The new branch's baseline takes those
tables' entries from the kept baseline.  A commit that stops being kept is
retired rather than let go of, and its pages are let go of by a later keep
once the retiring transaction has committed.

Kept commits are kept states of the materialization cache
(prolly_matcache.h), beside kept checkouts: the cache's visit() gives
each branch's kept commits (the refs' kept_commits(), each recording the
transaction that kept it, which a snapshot must see committed: refs
format 9) with their commits' roots, and every user of kept states takes both kinds.  A
first use at a kept commit is made of its pages, found through the cache.
A move of any branch (a reset, a checkout's move, a first use's) gives a
table whose rows differ from the target's, where a kept commit of any
branch holds the target's table and sharing costs less than writing the
rows, and the transaction that kept it precedes every snapshot in the
cluster, storage made of that commit's snapshots (branch operations'
share_kept(): O(1) each, the source branch held against deletion and its
relations against letting go of the pages, each relation stored alike on
both and the source's still on the storage that kept it), its baseline
entry taken from the kept baseline; and the move planner costs a first
use's candidates with those tables shared, as it does a kept checkout's.
So the old heads a branch has moved on from serve other branches until
they retire.  (The pages are the source branch's: a snapshot older than
their keeping could see versions it made and deleted before the commit,
rows in no commit; while one is open the rows are written instead.)

Kept materializations (prolly.kept_checkouts, 4 by default, per database)
make a later checkout of the commit a branch switch, its dependencies
checked as a first use checks them.  At a first use, the move planner
takes a kept one as any other branch, by its root, and costs every
candidate's copies with the tables a kept one holds as the target does
shared rather than written (a copy the planner empties to reload is
shared into too), so the copies chosen are those cheapest to finish.  A
checkout's sweep lets go of those
past the budget, the least recently checked out first (recency is kept in
shared memory; after a restart, older branches go first).  The budget
counts idle ones: one a session is on is neither counted nor let go of,
nor is the one a detached checkout's sweep is on.  One another sweep let
go of meanwhile counts as gone, so two sweeps at once let go of no more
than one would.  Letting go of one is deleting its branch, which
releases its relations' maps.  Writer branches done with are swept as
before, apart from the budget.  When something depends on a role being
dropped, DROP ROLE lets go of its database's idle kept checkouts and
checks again; another database's are let go of only from that database
(the error then has a hint).

With dolt.transaction_commit on, a transaction that changed a branch makes
its commit as it commits: the refs, opened by its first write, call
prolly_refs_end_hook at their pre-commit, before the chunk store's own, and
the commit is made there (none if nothing changed since the head).  It is
the transaction's own row changes on the head as it is then: heap reports
the TIDs of the tuple versions each write made or replaced
(branch_tuple_hook), and the change finder applies those the transaction
made and didn't replace, or replaced and hadn't made, to the head's table
trees (fold_rows), with no fold and no snapshot checked.  A transaction
that changed definitions, or more rows than it keeps (or than
maintenance_work_mem holds), or whose rows can't be applied so (a table
the head describes otherwise), commits its working set instead.
Such a commit descends the same trees many times (the refs' root for each
entry it gets and puts, the head's commit and root, each row's path for its
seek and then its apply), so it opens a tree keep_nodes() scope: each node
above the leaves, and each root, is read from the chunk store once (up to
maintenance_work_mem of them), and a
3-row commit reads about its rows' paths.  An apply keeps them so on its
own too, as each level's pass starts from the root.

A fold isn't made again while nothing it reads has changed: the refs
(refs_generations.c) keep in shared memory each branch's generation,
moved as a transaction that wrote the branch ends (every writer is told of
through branch.c), an epoch moved by every catalog change sent and every
collection, and the root the last fold of the branch made, known once its
transaction commits.  Reading the working set (status, diff, a branch's
dirtiness) takes that root while the generation and epoch stay and the
reader's snapshot sees every writer that ended.
debug_prolly_verify_fold_caches folds again and compares.

The fold worker (prolly_folder.c, prolly_fold_worker) takes a working-set
checkpoint of each branch written, in the background, so a status or a
commit folds only what came since, by default at every pass.  With
prolly_fold_worker_batch_rows set, a branch whose writes go on is folded
once its writers since its last fold wrote prolly_fold_worker_batch_rows
rows (the refs count them, each row a write changes) or the first of them
waited prolly_fold_worker_max_delay; one whose writes stopped (nobody
writing it, and no writer ended for a naptime) at the next pass, as is one
no writer of which ended since (nothing to batch). A fold writes the nodes
on the paths to the rows it changes, so folds of a few rows each write the
upper nodes again: batching writes keeps a busy branch's chunk writes, WAL
and journal near its rows' own, while a commit or status during the writes
folds the batch not yet folded itself (so batching is off by default).
pg_stat_prolly counts the worker's folds (fold_worker_folds), the
branches it left for a later pass (fold_worker_deferred), and its
attempts rolled back, the refs held or the branch moved meanwhile, to be
made again (fold_worker_retries), each branch's tries backed off (after
1, 3, 7... passes, 63 at most).  The launcher takes the databases written
in turn, and a worker exits after 64 passes, so a database written
without pause doesn't keep a slot from the others.  Autovacuum's
automatic fold (prolly.autofold_threshold), the backstop for the
database's default branch, folds the same way, before taking the refs,
and gives up while another holds them: a commit never waits for it.

A rewrite of a versioned table (ALTER TABLE changing a column's type,
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW) reads and writes every row: as it ends, the
branch is folded as a checkpoint (prolly.fold_on_rewrite, prolly_vcs.c's
prolly_fold_rewritten(), called by tablecmds.c and matview.c), so the
commit after, the fold worker off or in the same transaction, folds only
what changed since.  The checkpoint never waits for a lock (the rewrite
holds its table exclusively, and a commit holding the branch may be about
to read it): a lock not free at once skips it, leaving the table's next
fold to read it in full.  So does any other error the fold meets (it
folds every table of the branch, and one may hold what a commit refuses),
logged with the error's fields; a cancel, or an internal error (class
XX), fails the rewrite instead.  pg_stat_prolly's
rewrite_checkpoints_skipped counts the checkpoints skipped.  dolt_pending_full_folds() lists the tables whose next
fold would read them in full, and why, from the baseline, the catalogs and
each change finder's full_reason(), reading no rows.

A move of the session's branch (a reset, a fast-forward, a merge's)
folds its working set, if not known, as a checkpoint: the fold is the
branch's baseline.  After the rows are written, the baseline is advanced
to the target without another fold, each table the move wrote holding
the target's rows and its change log's record of the move's own writes
consumed (the canonicalizer's advance_baseline()); where it can't be (the
definitions moved too, a materialized view's rows did, a table's state
can't be advanced), the rest is folded as before.  So a move from a dirty
working set folds it once.

That fold is only compared with: once the baseline is advanced, it names
the target's trees, not the fold's.  So the fold, and every tree the move
builds on it, is kept in the backend's memory, not put in the chunk store
(chunkstore_local.c, refs_copies.c's settle_compared()).  Between
prolly_local_chunks_begin() and _end(), the canonicalizer's environments
put their chunks in a table in the backend's memory, and every
environment of the server's trees (prolly_folded_tree_env()'s) gets them
from there; their has() and put() stay the store's, so a durable tree
never takes a chunk only the backend has for stored.  The baseline is
kept beside the refs meanwhile (the refs' chunks are the store's), the
fold isn't published to the fold caches, and the value memo remembers
nothing from it.  At the end, prolly_local_chunks_persist() puts those of
the backend's chunks the baseline reaches, descending only into chunks
the store hasn't: its own few nodes when it was advanced, the fold's
trees it still names where it couldn't be (as before).  So a hard reset
of 1,000 dirty rows over 100,000 appends 4 chunks, not about 1,250.  The
table is bounded by maintenance_work_mem: past it, what it keeps is
persisted and the rest of the step puts in the store as before (a fold of
most of a table, which the baseline mostly can't be advanced past
anyway).  A tree only the backend keeps may still take a stored value's
tree by its TOAST pointer, or the memo's reference to one: each is stored,
which is where persisting stops.

What a fold works out for every table, changed or not, is cached in the
backend (canon_folded.c's own): each table's description and the hash its
baseline is kept by, dropped by a relcache invalidation of the table or a
syscache one of anything a description names; the tree of what versioned
objects depend on, against an epoch every such callback moves (inval.c
sends a relcache invalidation of pg_depend or pg_extension themselves for
their changes, which have no syscache); which objects are extensions'
members; and the last canonical tree's items, so the next is applied as
the items that differ (the baseline's too, from the baseline folded from).
debug_prolly_verify_fold_caches works each one taken out again too.

A fold after another in the same backend works out again only what may
have changed (fold_incremental): with no catalog change or collection
since (the refs' mark, moved by every catalog change sent, and the
backend's own catalog epoch), the same deparsing settings, and a snapshot
that sees every writer of the branch that ended, it takes the last
canonical tree's items and redoes those of the tables whose rows were
written since, and every sequence's (their values change outside
transactions), reading no catalog through.  The refs learn which tables:
every row a writer changes names its relation (note_relation()), and as
the writer ends the branch keeps them in shared memory, the last few with
the generation they ended at (refs_generations.c).  A writer of too many
relations, an eviction, a prepared transaction's end or anything else the
mark doesn't cover folds everything.  A commit's fold begins the next
baseline from the one this backend built last, only the written tables'
entries changed.  debug_prolly_verify_fold_caches then folds everything
else again (its work not counted) and compares.  dolt_status reads definitions
without rows and asks each table's change finder (from that table's
baseline entry alone), so a status in full visits every table; but the
definitions are those the last read here made while nothing they're of
changed (no catalog change in this backend's epoch, the same deparsing
settings and baseline, the tree still stored), only every sequence's item
worked out again (pg_stat_prolly's definitions_reused).  They're kept
apart from the folds' items, so a status between two commits leaves the
second's fold to work out only what was written.
A later status takes the last one's answer too, when it compared the same
two roots (the version's and the definitions', which holds every table's
description and baseline rows) and the refs know which tables were written
since a mark it took before asking any change finder (written_since()):
only those tables are compared again (pg_stat_prolly's status_reused), so
a status after a row write costs about the tables written, not every
table.  The answer is this backend's own, and is kept only from a
comparison of every table's changes read so: a table whose change finder
can't tell its changes that way (a keyless or partitioned one) makes every
status a fold, and status_reused never moves.
debug_prolly_verify_fold_caches reads them all again and compares.


Remotes
-------

Transfer ("chunks", transfer_chunks.c) moves the chunks a commit reaches
that the other side lacks, re-hashing each chunk it receives.  A remote
("file", remote_file.c) is a directory of table files and a manifest.
What a transfer has seen (prolly_seen.c) is a map held to its share of
maintenance_work_mem: past it, sorted runs in temporary files, merged
four of a level into one, each with a Bloom filter folded smaller as the
map grows, so its memory stays within budget and a lookup checks a number
of runs logarithmic in its size.

Tags are objects of their own, as commits are: a small tree naming the
commit, its tagger, date and message, whose hash is the tag's id, so a tag
is the same in every database.  The refs name each tag's object and keep
its commit beside it, so resolving a tag reads no object.  A push of a tag
sends its object and what that reaches, then moves the remote's tag; a
fetch follows the remote's tags of commits it has (as Dolt's does), and a
clone takes every tag.  A file remote's manifest lists tags on lines of
their own.

The refs tree records its format, and a file remote's manifest its
version; while the prototype iterates, neither is kept readable across a
change: refs or a manifest of another format are refused, never read.


SQL
---

prolly_dolt.c is the dolt_* functions and system views, with Doltgres's
arguments and results, so scripts written for Doltgres run here.  A tag
(dolt_tag, the dolt_tags view) is a revision wherever one is read, after a
branch of its name; checking one out, as checking out a commit's hash, is a
read-only detached session at its commit.

dolt_rebase replays a branch's commits onto another, as Dolt's: on a
branch of its own, dolt_rebase_<branch>, made at the commit replayed onto,
whose rebase state (the plan, the branch rebased and its head then) the
refs keep with that branch.  Each step is a cherry-pick; a reword, squash
or fixup then replaces the pick's commit with one of its working set, and
a commit that became empty is dropped (unless --empty=keep).  The plan is
edited through the dolt_rebase view, whose INSTEAD OF trigger changes the
state.  A step that conflicts stops the rebase as a pick in progress
(outside a transaction block, an error, which rolls back the statement:
a rebase it began is gone, one begun before is left as it was, to
continue or abort), and --continue commits its resolution; a pick whose
definitions conflict (a schema conflict) is such an error too.  The last
step done, the rebased branch is moved to the rebase's head (refused if
it moved meanwhile), and the rebase's branch deleted.  An edit's stop
may amend the commit it stopped at: dolt_commit('--amend') replaces the
head commit with one of the working set and the head's parents, its
message kept unless -m gives one, as Dolt's, refused with constraint
violations unless forced as a commit is.  An amend and a rebase's steps
replace commits from the head they read, so they hold the branch first,
and fail (40001, to retry) if another's commit moved it meanwhile; the
commit amended away has its kept pages let go of, the commit before it
staying kept.
prolly_vcs.c adds prolly_root_hash() and prolly_checkpoint().

Snapshot mounts (prolly_snapshot.c) are history read through the
planner: prolly_mount(ref, schema, target) resolves the ref to one commit
and makes, in the ordinary schema target, a foreign table (foreign data
wrapper prolly_snapshot, server prolly_local, made at the first mount)
for each table of schema the commit has, defined as the commit describes
it: columns, types and collations, no constraints.  Mounting reads the
commit's root items alone (the canonicalizer's table_metadata()): no row
is read, re-sorted or written.  It refuses what can't be read as stored:
a key ordered by a type, operator class or collation that isn't built in,
a collation whose version changed, an extension ordering it that changed.
The refs pin the commit under the mount's name ('P', the refs' format
10), so a collection keeps what it reaches; prolly_unmount() and
prolly_refresh_mount() drop the mount's tables first, so they wait for
its scans.  A scan reads only the commit its mount pins, rechecks that
the reader may read the table's history (SELECT on the table of its name,
row security not applying), and streams the data tree in key order:
equalities on the primary key's leading columns and a range on the next
seek and stop the scan (the keying's encode_key_bound()), the key's order
is offered to the planner both ways, and an equality joined to another
relation gives a parameterized path that re-seeks on each rescan.  Only
the columns the query uses are decoded.  The quals are all kept as the
plan's filter too.

Commits don't hold secondary indexes.  A mount made with indexes
(prolly_mount(..., indexes => true)) builds a tree of its own for each
btree index the version describes (not a partial or expression index, nor
one ordered by what isn't built in): an entry per row, keyed by the
index's columns under the version's operator families, collations and
options, then the row's data-tree key, holding the row with only the
index's, INCLUDE and primary key columns (the value encoder's project(),
made of the row's bytes).  The pin keeps those trees too (refs format 11:
a pin's kept trees).  An index scan seeks and orders as the primary key's
does, and reads a row from the data tree only where its entry lacks a
column the query uses.  A refresh brings each tree to the new commit by
the data trees' differences when the rows are keyed and laid out alike,
else builds it again; the first build sorts every row's entry in memory
(maintenance_work_mem).  A plain mount builds none and reads no row.

A history mount (prolly_mount(..., history => true)) is dolt_history_table()
as a foreign table: each table's columns as the mounted commit has them,
then commit_hash, committer and commit_date, with a row for each row at
each commit the mounted one reaches (each commit once, merges' parents
too), read as dolt_history_table(NULL::t) reads it: the commit's table
of the name (a table renamed since isn't followed to its old name, as the
functions don't follow it), its columns by identity (one renamed since by
its name there, one retyped since converted, a value that doesn't convert
NULL with a warning once a scan, one the commit hasn't NULL).  A commit
whose table can't be read here (its key ordered by what isn't built in)
is an error, as a commit missing from the store is.  A reader other than
the database's owner or pg_read_all_data is refused a commit whose table
of the name isn't the one here (not made from its relation, walked once a
scan), as dolt_history_table() by name refuses it.  A scan reads a
commit at a time and streams its rows, so LIMIT stops it.  An equality
on commit_hash (a constant, a parameter, or a join's outer row) reads
that commit alone, if the mounted commit reaches it: the history is
walked highest commit first down to that commit's height, and what is
found is kept for the scan's next rescan, so one lookup reads the
commits above it and a join reads each commit once.  The primary key's
bounds seek in each commit whose key is the version's, and a commit keyed
otherwise is read whole.  Planning reads no history (the commits are
estimated by the mounted one's height).  prolly_mounts() shows which
mounts are history mounts.  The dolt_history_table() functions still read
every commit.


Core hooks
----------

prolly_shmem.c is a shared-memory subsystem (subsystemlist.h) reserving
an area per chunk store, value memo and refs implementation that asks for
one, and CheckPointProlly() runs in CheckPointGuts(), and
ProllyRecoveryConsistent() beside XLogCheckInvalidPages(), as recovery
becomes consistent; the chunk store asks XLogBackupInProgress() (xlog.c)
before it removes a file a backup's redo may copy from.  Every batch of
shared invalidation messages sent calls prolly_forget_folds() (sinval.c).
createdb() calls
ProllyCopyDatabase() (prolly_database.c) to copy the template's chunk
store.  A temporary or unlogged table is never stored by prolly
(prolly_table_am_for_persistence()).


Tests
-----

src/test/modules/test_prolly runs each interface's conformance suite
against every registered implementation, and tests version control from
SQL and TAP:

	make -C src/test/modules/test_prolly check

src/test/prolly runs Postgres's own regression suites, sqllogictest and
Dolt's scenarios against prolly tables.
